  "findings": [
    "User activity is strongly long-tailed: median 4 events against a mean of 14.0, reaching 7,436. Averages alone are misleading here; report percentiles.",
    "Purchases are 1.75% of events, 11.5% of users and 6.8% of sessions. T2 will be heavily imbalanced; rank-aware metrics such as PR-AUC fit, accuracy does not.",
    "691,246 users (22.9%) have exactly one event and 1,475,463 (48.8%) have exactly one session. Neither group carries a sequence to learn from.",
    "35.4% of sessions hold exactly one event, so a large share of sessions carry no within-session sequence.",
    "category_code is missing on 31.84% of rows while category_id is complete. Modelling can use the 624 ids, but a demo cannot show a readable name for every event.",
    "The event vocabulary is view, cart and purchase only. There is no remove-from-cart event, so no downstream rule may assume one exists.",
    "Null sessions are extremely rare (2 rows) but not zero, so session handling still needs a defined path for them.",
    "1,575 multi-event sessions occur at a single instant, and 35,207 events repeat exactly. Ordering must use the source_row_number tie-break rather than the timestamp alone.",
    "Median user is active on 1 distinct day(s) while the median calendar span is 0.01 days. Span overstates engagement and should not be used as an eligibility signal on its own.",
    "The most active user holds 7,436 events and the largest session holds 1,159. These need a downstream policy review before training, but nothing is excluded in this task."
  ]
}